  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1149 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nClear and calm weather resides through much of the southern and\ncentral CONUS this evening. The trough responsible for the\npattern change sits to our east with a cold front well into the\nGulf with an area of high pressure centered over the Midwest.\nContinued height rises will promote more subsidence into Sunday,\nleading to clear weather and higher surface pressure to continue\nthrough tonight with light northerly winds. This sets the stage\nfor optimal radiational cooling conditions that will lead to the\ncoolest low temperatures we have seen in months. HREF guidance\nstill paints large areas where lows have greater than a 50%\nchance of lows below 37 F this morning, leading to the\npossibility of isolated, patchy frost to develop overnight.\n\nMore of the same, in terms of the daytime temperature forecast,\nis expected Sunday with highs reaching into the low 70s with\nlight winds. The surface high to our north will start to weaken\nand shift east at the same time, bringing southerlies back into\nthe forecast briefly on Monday. However, a strong surface cyclone\nwill develop off the east coast, forcing the cessation of\nsoutherly winds and bringing winds to near calm. Therefore, the\nrest of this week looks to be more calm, dry weather with a\ngradual warming trend as advective processes remain weak. Highs\nwill creep back into the 80s by Wednesday across northern\nMississippi, eventually creeping further north with each day\nthrough the end of the forecast. So, expect average temperatures\nbecoming slightly above average with a dry forecast through this\nweek.",
